Johnston Engineering collaborated with our client to develop a containerized direct air capture (DAC) pilot plant designed for accelerated lifetime testing of absorbent media. This pilot plant was created to validate the long-term performance of the absorbent media under various operating conditions, ensuring its effectiveness and durability for commercial deployment. The DAC pilot plant was designed to handle various stressors and simulate long-term operational conditions to accelerate the testing process. This enabled the client to quickly gather critical data on the absorbent media's performance and make informed decisions for future development and deployment.

This project was deployed in May of 2024 and is successfully operating in Colorado. 

**Client Goal:**

- Validate long-term performance of absorbent media

- Conduct accelerated lifetime testing

**Johnston Engineering Services Provided:**

Johnston Engineering provided the complete design, analysis, and construction of the DAC pilot plant, which included approximately 5,000 custom and off-the-shelf parts. Our comprehensive services ensured the project's success and the client's objectives were met.

- Design:

- Mechanical design of ~5,000 parts, including machined components, sheet metal, weldments, and 80/20 frames.

- Development of detailed 3D CAD models and assembly drawings.

- Structural Analysis:

- Analysis of structural frames to ensure stability and integrity under pressure and vacuum conditions.

- Detailed stress and load analysis to verify the strength and durability of the frames and components.

- Thermal Analysis:

- Steady-state and transient thermal analysis to optimize the thermal performance of the system.

- Custom cold plate design for rapid changes in temperature needed for temperature swing absorption cycle.

- Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD):

- Fluid distribution analysis to ensure uniform flow over the absorption media of the DAC system.

- Build and Assembly:

- Fabrication and assembly of the entire system inside a 40ft shipping container, ensuring compact and efficient packaging on-site at Johnston Engineering. 

- Quoting, purchasing, and inspection of all external vendor fabricated components. 

- Coordination of all fabrication and subcontractor activities to meet project timelines and budget.

##  Point Source CO2 Capture – Test Fixture Design & Build for Media Characterization Study

Johnston Engineering assisted with the research, commercialization, design, Finite Element Analysis (FEA), and Computational Fluid Dynamics (CFD) to successfully develop a decarbonization device meant for use in commercial buildings, for our client Carbon Quest. This project was deployed in 2022 and the product is now installed in a High Rise in Times Square, NY. 

CarbonQuest has developed a modular system that allows for the capture and liquefaction of CO2 on-site. "This recycled CO2 is then made available to off-takers who can use it in a variety of environmentally beneficial ways, such as in the production of concrete or as a replacement for petroleum in jet fuel and chemical processes. By implementing this innovative system, building owners and operators can now enjoy a new revenue stream while simultaneously ensuring compliance with emissions guidelines" - Carbon Quest.

**Johnston Engineering Services Provided:**

Johnston Engineering provided mechanical engineering design, thermal analysis, two-phase pressure drop analysis, component sourcing, build and testing services. We provided design, modifications, operation, and data processing of a research and development test fixture to characterize CO2 absorbent performance in a pressure swing absorption cycle. 

- Mechanical Design

- Mechanical Engineering

- Convection Thermal Analysis

- Two-phase pressure drop analysis

- Material Sourcing

- Material Ordering

- Product Assembly

- Product Test

The purpose of the CO2 absorbent performance R&D test fixture was to characterize the performance of various candidate absorbents for use in a pressure-swing absorption cycle to concentrate CO2 gas. A P&ID diagram of the test fixture was created to identify all components in the system.  Characteristic equations were developed to quantify system and key component uncertainties such as mass flow of CO2 in mixed gas flow, and total mass flow of CO2 leaving the system. 3D CAD files were generated in SOLIDWORKS and reviewed with the customer to ensure the proper design intent was met. Pressure, flow and temperature sensors were selected, then we built the system and wrote the LabVIEW data acquisition code. We performed tests, data reduction, and data analysis on several absorbents. The test result was highly improved CO2 concentration performance.

In another portion of the development, Johnston Engineering worked with CarbonQuest on the two-phase pressure drop in a line containing flowing liquid CO2 and noncondensable gas.  CarbonQuest needed to know the pressure drop in this line to ensure proper system operation. Calculation during two-phase flow requires additional work compared to single-phase flow. REFPROPS data was used for fluid properties and the Martinelli Nelson parameter was used for Turbulent vapor Turbulent liquid flows assuming the separated flow model.  The Chisholm and Laird two-phase multiplier was used in the calculation to relate the frictional component of a two-phase pressure drop to a single-phase pressure drop at the same mass flow rate to calculate the overall line pressure drop.  All engineering efforts were made on behalf of Carbon Quest and their new patented, ground-breaking technology.

# Nitto Mobile Demonstration Unit Scope of Work

Johnston Engineering will provide complete design, analysis, build and test services for a mobile, containerized demonstration unit intended to demonstrate the performance and effectiveness of Nitto Denko Corporation’s proprietary spiral-wound CO₂ separation membrane technology. The scope of work includes the design, fabrication, assembly, and testing of a single-stage membrane-based carbon capture system with an inlet flow from a flue gas stream.

The system will be designed for mobile deployment at customer sites and will incorporate thermal management, moisture control and instrumentation as indicated in the Nitto provided P&ID. While only a single-stage system is included in this scope, the system layout, structure, piping, electrical, and controls architecture will be intentionally designed to allow a future second-stage membrane system to be integrated without requiring redesign of the base system.

Johnston Engineering will execute this effort as a complete design, analysis, build and test project, converting client-provided 2D drawings and process information into a fully fabricated, tested mobile demonstration unit suitable for repeated deployment.

## Assumptions 

- Fluid properties calculated using NIST REFPROP 9 using CO2, H2O, O2 and N2 concentrations at appropriate temperatures and pressure when possible. Viscosity and thermal conductivity calculated using air at appropriate 

- Notes in VP1 indicate 0C and 1 ATM are “normal conditions” for calculating flow rates  

- During Questions and Answer period, we understand that Johnston Engineering should propose sensors with slightly lower accuracy with the benefit of cost savings.  We propose pressure sensors combined linearity, hysteresis and repeatability +/-  0.08%, and flow sensors with 0.4% reading +0.2% full scale. 

- Testing will occur at Johnston Engineering’s facilities and is intended to confirm the signal of sensors and valves operation with the control system, and is not intended to be connected to a flue gas stream or include gas analysis equipment. 

